<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Android 13+ will not show a single notification until the user has
         granted this at runtime. Declaring it is only half the job: the prompt
         is spent deliberately, behind the rationale sheet, because the system
         gives an app exactly one. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>
    <!-- Only read when the user turns on "Use address book names". -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CONTACTS"/>
    <!-- The default-SMS-app role. All four are declared together deliberately:
         Android decides whether Weft is even *eligible* for the role by looking
         for the whole set — the two receivers, the service and these
         permissions — and an app missing one of them simply never appears in
         the "Default SMS app" picker. There is no error, no log line and no
         failed call to debug; the entry is absent. Declaring them does not
         grant them: holding ROLE_SMS is what grants the SMS group at runtime,
         which is why `SmsRole.hasPermissions` is reported to Dart separately
         from `isDefault`. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_SMS"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SEND_SMS"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_SMS"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_MMS"/>
    <!-- The fifth, and the one whose absence produced the whole "Nothing
         changed" bug. It is not about receiving a WAP push — `MmsReceiver` is
         reached through `RECEIVE_MMS` above — it is about being *allowed to be
         the default app at all*.

         When the role is granted, AOSP's `SmsApplication` assigns the app-ops
         the role carries: OP_WRITE_SMS, OP_RECEIVE_SMS, OP_RECEIVE_MMS,
         OP_SEND_SMS, OP_READ_SMS and OP_RECEIVE_WAP_PUSH. An app-op cannot be
         granted for a permission the package never declared, so the last one
         silently fails and `SmsApplication` then *disqualifies* the app for
         having "lost" a required op. The visible result is the one that was
         reported: `RoleManager` says Sennit holds ROLE_SMS, and
         `Telephony.Sms.getDefaultSmsPackage()` — which is what the app, the
         provider and every other app read — still names the previous holder.
         Nothing throws. The only trace is one logcat line from a system class:

             E SmsApplication: com.bostrot.weft lost android:receive_wap_push:
             (no permission to fix)

         Found by running the role request on a device, not by reading the
         code; `sms_manifest_test.dart` now holds it. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_WAP_PUSH"/>
    <!-- And the sixth, for exactly the same reason and found the same way:
         with RECEIVE_WAP_PUSH added, the role was granted and the default
         still did not move, and the log line moved on to the next op in
         `SmsApplication`'s list:

             E SmsApplication: com.bostrot.weft lost
             android:read_cell_broadcasts: (no permission to fix)

         The set is all-or-nothing. Weft does not read cell broadcasts and has
         no screen for emergency alerts; the permission is declared because the
         *role* carries the op, and an app that cannot hold every op the role
         assigns cannot hold the role in the way that matters — which is the
         only way `Telephony.Sms.getDefaultSmsPackage()` will name it. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CELL_BROADCASTS"/>
    <!-- Calling. `MANAGE_OWN_CALLS` is what lets Weft register a *self-managed*
         phone account and hand Telecom a call of its own; it is a normal
         permission, granted at install, and without it `addNewIncomingCall`
         throws `SecurityException` rather than failing quietly. `RECORD_AUDIO`
         and `CAMERA` are the dangerous pair WebRTC actually opens — requested at
         runtime, at the moment a call is placed or answered, because a
         permission sheet in front of a ringing phone is the only place either
         question makes sense.

         `USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT` is what makes an incoming call take over a
         locked screen instead of arriving as a card behind it. From Android 14
         the system grants it only to apps whose function is calling or alarms,
         which a self-managed `ConnectionService` app is — and revokes it from
         everyone else, which is exactly why the notification is posted under
         `CATEGORY_CALL` and by a calling app rather than as one more message.

         The two `FOREGROUND_SERVICE` entries go together: from Android 14 a
         foreground service must declare a type *and* hold the permission that
         matches it, and `phoneCall` additionally requires a Telecom call to be
         in progress. See `CallForegroundService`. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_OWN_CALLS"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO"/>
    <!-- Only requested the moment the user taps "share location". -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_FULL_SCREEN_INTENT"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_PHONE_CALL"/>
    <!-- The built-in background connection: a specialUse foreground service is
         the one documented way an app whose purpose is staying reachable may
         hold a connection, and specialUse (unlike dataSync) may still be
         started from BOOT_COMPLETED on Android 15. The battery-exemption
         permission only enables *asking* — the user answers a system dialog,
         and saying no costs delivery during Doze, not the feature. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE"/>
    <!-- Long jobs the user started: a backup, a restore, attachments still
         uploading, the search backfill. `dataSync` is the type for exactly
         that, and like every type it needs its own permission from Android 14.
         See `AndroidBackgroundWorkHost`. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS"/>
    <!-- Bluetooth headsets are a call audio *route*, and from Android 12 the
         route cannot even be enumerated without this. Declared with
         `neverForLocation` because Weft has no interest in where the user is and
         the permission would otherwise imply it. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_CONNECT"/>
    <!-- A phone with no camera can still take a voice call, and a tablet with no
         microphone is not a device this refuses to install on. Both are declared
         optional so the Play listing does not exclude hardware that works. -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false"/>
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.microphone" android:required="false"/>
    <!-- l10n-ignore: the app's own name, never translated. -->
    <application
        android:label="Sennit"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:localeConfig="@xml/locales_config">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Custom scheme: weft://chat/<id> etc. Works with no domain
                 verification, so it is the always-available fallback. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="weft"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Matrix room links: `matrix:r/room:server` and matrix.to. Not
                 verified — matrix.to is nobody's own domain, so Android offers
                 Sennit beside the browser rather than instead of it. The app
                 opens the room, or asks before joining one. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="matrix"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="matrix.to"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Verified App Links for weft.bostrot.com. Requires
                 tooling/web/assetlinks.json served at
                 https://weft.bostrot.com/.well-known/assetlinks.json with the
                 release signing key's SHA-256. -->
            <intent-filter android:autoVerify="true">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="https" android:host="weft.bostrot.com"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- "Send a message to this number", from the dialer, contacts, a
                 web page's `sms:` link or another app's `Intent.ACTION_SENDTO`.
                 Required of the default SMS app — an app that cannot be handed
                 a recipient this way is not eligible for the role — and useful
                 long before Weft holds it: the same filter is how a tel-adjacent
                 link opens a Weft composer at all.

                 All four schemes, because callers are inconsistent about which
                 they use and the pairs are not synonyms to the intent resolver:
                 `sms:`/`mms:` are "the conversation with", `smsto:`/`mmsto:`
                 are "compose to". BROWSABLE is what lets a link in a browser
                 reach it. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SENDTO"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="sms"/>
                <data android:scheme="smsto"/>
                <data android:scheme="mms"/>
                <data android:scheme="mmsto"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Share-in: Weft appears in every app's share sheet for the
                 content types a messenger can forward. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/*"/>
                <data android:mimeType="video/*"/>
                <data android:mimeType="audio/*"/>
                <data android:mimeType="application/pdf"/>
                <data android:mimeType="text/x-vcard"/>
                <data android:mimeType="application/*"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/*"/>
                <data android:mimeType="video/*"/>
                <data android:mimeType="*/*"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Direct Share. The filters above put *Weft* in the share sheet;
                 this puts the user's actual conversations there, as faces above
                 the app row. The XML declares which types can be shared to a
                 conversation and under which category; the conversations
                 themselves are dynamic shortcuts published at runtime by
                 `ShareShortcuts.kt`.

                 Note this is meta-data on the activity, not a category on the
                 filters. `android.shortcut.conversation` is not an intent
                 category — no sharing app calls `addCategory` with it — so it
                 lives on the shortcut and on the `<share-target>` in
                 res/xml/shortcuts.xml, which is what the system reads. -->
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.shortcuts"
                android:resource="@xml/shortcuts"/>
        </activity>
        <!-- Sign-in in the person's own browser (BrowserAuth.kt). The
             invisible activity that opens the page and waits for it.
             `singleTask` with MainActivity's (empty) affinity: the redirect
             arrives in the browser's task, and starting this from there must
             come back to the one copy in Sennit's task, closing the tab. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".BrowserAuthActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|screenLayout|density|uiMode"/>
        <!-- Where a browser without Auth Tabs sends Mattermost's mobile SSO
             redirect, and a Matrix server's OAuth or SSO sign-in. Disabled
             here and enabled only while a sign-in waits: `mmauth` is also the
             official Mattermost app's scheme, and Sennit must not offer to
             open sign-ins it did not start. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".BrowserAuthCallbackActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:noHistory="true"
            android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="mmauth" android:host="callback"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!-- Matrix sign-in (`kOAuthRedirectUri`): no host, because an
                 OAuth server refuses a private-use redirect that has one. -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>
                <data android:scheme="com.erictrenkel.sennit"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- SMS delivery. `SMS_DELIVER` is broadcast to exactly one app on the
             device — the default one — and it is the *only* way that app is
             told about a message. `SMS_RECEIVED`, the one every tutorial shows,
             is the read-only broadcast every other app gets, and it does not
             carry the obligation that comes with the role: the default app owns
             writing the message into the system provider, and no other app on
             the device will do it. A default SMS app that skips that write is
             not merely impolite — the user's messages simply cease to exist for
             every other app, backup and future default.

             `android:permission` on the receiver is the other half of the
             contract: it declares that only a sender holding BROADCAST_SMS —
             i.e. the system — may deliver this intent, which is what stops any
             installed app from forging an inbound message. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".sms.SmsReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BROADCAST_SMS">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.provider.Telephony.SMS_DELIVER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
        <receiver
            android:name=".sms.MmsReceiver"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BROADCAST_WAP_PUSH">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.provider.Telephony.WAP_PUSH_DELIVER"/>
                <data android:mimeType="application/vnd.wap.mms-message"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
        <!-- Delivery reports and send outcomes come back as broadcasts, not as
             a callback on the send call: `SmsManager.sendTextMessage` takes two
             PendingIntents and returns void. Not exported — the only sender is
             this app's own PendingIntent. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".sms.SmsStatusReceiver"
            android:exported="false"/>
        <!-- "Respond via message" from the incoming-call screen. The dialer
             starts this while Weft is not running and often while the screen is
             locked, so it sends in Kotlin and never wakes Flutter: an engine
             start would take longer than the call rings. -->
        <service
            android:name=".sms.HeadlessSmsSendService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.SEND_RESPOND_VIA_MESSAGE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.RESPOND_VIA_MESSAGE"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <data android:scheme="sms"/>
                <data android:scheme="smsto"/>
                <data android:scheme="mms"/>
                <data android:scheme="mmsto"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- Calling. Telecom binds this to build a `Connection` for every call,
             incoming or outgoing, and it binds it *itself* — which is why the
             whole call bridge is written to work with no activity and no Flutter
             engine in the process.

             `android:permission` here is the contract in the other direction: it
             declares that only the system, which holds `BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE`,
             may bind this. Without it any installed app could bind the service
             and manufacture a call. Exported because the binder is the system,
             in another process. -->
        <service
            android:name=".call.WeftConnectionService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_TELECOM_CONNECTION_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.telecom.ConnectionService"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </service>
        <!-- What keeps a backgrounded call alive through Doze. Not exported:
             the only thing that starts it is `WeftConnection`, in this process,
             immediately after Telecom has created the call the `phoneCall` type
             requires. -->
        <service
            android:name=".call.CallForegroundService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="phoneCall"/>
        <!-- flutter_local_notifications' own service, started by
             `AndroidBackgroundWorkHost` while a long job runs so it survives the
             user leaving the app. Does no work itself; its notification is the
             job's progress. Stops with the task: swiping Sennit away ends the
             engine the job runs in, and a notification outliving it would
             claim a backup that is no longer happening. -->
        <service
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ForegroundService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:stopWithTask="true"
            android:foregroundServiceType="dataSync"/>
        <!-- The built-in background connection. Holds the process (and the
             headless engine with the Matrix sync loop) alive behind a
             minimum-importance notification. See WeftSyncService. -->
        <service
            android:name=".sync.WeftSyncService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse">
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="Maintains the user's own messaging connection so end-to-end encrypted messages arrive while the app is closed; there is no push service in the self-hosted deployment this app targets."/>
        </service>
        <!-- Brings the connection back after a reboot, only when the user
             turned it on. Exported because BOOT_COMPLETED is a system
             broadcast.

             Three actions, not one. BOOT_COMPLETED is the documented one and
             the only one most devices send; both QUICKBOOT_POWERON spellings
             are what a number of vendor ROMs send *instead* after their
             "fast boot" shutdown, which on those phones is the ordinary way
             the device turns off. Listening for BOOT_COMPLETED alone means the
             app is never started on them, and it presents as notifications
             that stop after a restart until the app is opened by hand.

             No LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED and no android:directBootAware. Before the
             first unlock only device-protected storage is readable, and every
             thing this service needs — the Matrix session, the account's keys,
             the Olm store, and the preference saying the connection is wanted
             at all — is credential-protected. A receiver woken there would post
             a foreground notification, fail to sign in and stop. Moving the
             keys to device-protected storage to fix it would put an E2EE
             messenger's long-term secrets where the OS decrypts them before
             anyone has proved they own the phone. See SyncBootReceiver. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".sync.SyncBootReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
                <action android:name="com.htc.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
        <!-- Answer and decline on the incoming-call notification. A receiver
             rather than an activity so a decline costs no engine start: it hands
             the action to `CallBridge`, which replays it if Dart is not
             listening yet. Not exported — the only sender is this app's own
             PendingIntent, the same rule `SmsStatusReceiver` follows. -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".call.CallActionReceiver"
            android:exported="false"/>
        <!-- Scheduled local notifications: the "remind me" on a message and
             reply reminders. `zonedSchedule` sets an alarm whose broadcast
             goes to the first receiver; without it the alarm fires into
             nothing. The boot receiver puts pending ones back after a restart
             or an app update, which clear every alarm an app has set. -->
        <receiver
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationReceiver"
            android:exported="false"/>
        <receiver
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
                <action android:name="com.htc.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Home screen widgets. Both are `GlanceAppWidgetReceiver`s, which are
             `AppWidgetProvider`s underneath, which are `BroadcastReceiver`s —
             so what is declared here is the same three lines an AppWidget has
             always needed: the UPDATE action, and the `<meta-data>` pointing at
             the provider XML that describes the sizes.

             `exported="true"` is not optional and not a widening: the sender of
             ACTION_APPWIDGET_UPDATE is the system, in another process, and a
             non-exported provider is one the launcher can never place. It is
             also what lets `WeftSnapshotStore.notifySurfaces` redraw them the
             moment the payload changes — that broadcast is explicit, aimed at
             our own component, and is how a widget is current rather than up to
             an hour old.

             What a widget may *draw* is not decided here. The receivers read
             `WeftSnapshotStore.widgetsEnabled` on every update and render an
             empty state when it is false, so a placed widget on a phone whose
             owner never turned the setting on shows a sentence explaining that
             and no conversations. Fail-closed lives in Kotlin, not in the
             manifest, because the manifest cannot express "off until asked". -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.WeftUnreadWidgetReceiver"
            android:label="@string/weft_widget_unread_name"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/weft_widget_unread_info"/>
        </receiver>
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.WeftInboxWidgetReceiver"
            android:label="@string/weft_widget_inbox_name"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/weft_widget_inbox_info"/>
        </receiver>

        <!-- The Wear OS reply path. A `WearableListenerService` is started by
             the system when a message arrives on a path this filter names,
             whether or not Weft is running — which is why the reply path is a
             queue that reports rather than a call that returns: this component
             can be what starts the process, with no Flutter engine in it yet.

             `android:exported` true and the `com.google.android.gms.wearable.BIND_LISTENER`
             action are both required of the *host*: the binder is Play services,
             not another app, and a service without them is simply never bound —
             no error, no log line, and a watch whose replies vanish.

             The path filter is exact and names only the reply. The snapshot
             travels the other way as a `DataItem` this phone puts, so there is
             nothing inbound to listen for on it, and a `DATA_CHANGED` filter
             here would wake this process every time it published to itself. -->
        <service
            android:name=".wear.WeftWearReplyService"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="com.google.android.gms.wearable.MESSAGE_RECEIVED"/>
                <data android:scheme="wear" android:host="*" android:pathPrefix="/weft/reply"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- FCM. The service itself (`FlutterFirebaseMessagingService`) is
             declared by the firebase_messaging plugin and merged in from its
             own manifest — redeclaring it here would be a second entry for one
             service. What is *not* supplied by the plugin is where a message
             lands when Weft is not running to place it: without this channel
             id, Android drops such a notification into a "Miscellaneous"
             channel the user cannot tune and Weft does not know about. Weft's
             own pokes are data-only and are posted by Dart onto the per-network
             channels, so this is the fallback, not the normal path. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.default_notification_channel_id"
            android:value="weft.messages.matrix" />
        <!-- No FCM token until the user has chosen a gateway: `getToken()`
             is called only for Sennit's gateway or their own, and turning push
             off deletes the token again. Without this, Firebase would register
             with Google on the first launch, before the explanation screen. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.auto_init_enabled"
            android:value="false" />

        <!-- Android Auto. Declares Sennit a messaging app, which in the car
             means notifications and nothing else: the head unit reads the
             MessagingStyle notifications the phone posts, speaks them, and
             answers through the Reply and Mark-as-read actions. Those two run
             headless — the receiver below is what delivers them with Sennit
             closed — because a driver cannot be handed the phone's UI. -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="com.google.android.gms.car.application"
            android:resource="@xml/automotive_app_desc"/>
        <!-- flutter_local_notifications' receiver for actions that do not open
             the app (`showsUserInterface: false`): Reply and Mark read. The
             plugin does not declare it itself; without it the broadcast goes
             nowhere and the reply typed in the shade, on a watch or in the car
             is lost without a trace. Not exported — the only sender is this
             app's own PendingIntent. -->
        <receiver
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ActionBroadcastReceiver"
            android:exported="false"/>

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
        <!-- "Add to calendar". add_2_calendar only *starts* the editor if it
             can resolve it first, and package visibility hides the calendar
             app unless the intent is declared here — without this the tap
             silently does nothing, which is exactly how it presented. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.INSERT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="vnd.android.cursor.item/event"/>
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.INSERT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="vnd.android.cursor.dir/event"/>
        </intent>
        <!-- Sign-in in the person's own browser (BrowserAuth.kt): which
             browser can open a Custom Tab, and whether it can open an Auth
             Tab, are both asked of its Custom Tabs service. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.support.customtabs.action.CustomTabsService"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
